WEBVTT
TITLE Lexical Errors and Lexer Recovery: Longest Match, the Two Failures, Panic Mode and the Guard — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
A lexer reads characters and writes tokens. A token is a class plus the exact text it matched.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
It makes two decisions: where a token starts, and where it ends. The second one is the longest match.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
On x1 23mm 78 y 7z the longest match gives seven tokens, not five.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
So a lexical error is a position where no token can be formed. Everything else belongs to another phase.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
Here is the automaton for this language: 24 states, 18 of them accepting.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
Each state remembers how far into a token the lexer has read. Each accepting state names the class.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
It is deterministic: from every state, one character leads to at most one place. That is checked, not assumed.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
It agrees with an independently written matcher on all 2,955 strings of length up to three, and on all 13 corpus strings.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Longest match decides that a/*b*/c is two tokens and a / * b / c is six. The spaces change the program.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
The run passes an accepting state and keeps going: / is a token, but /* might be a longer one.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
A lexer that stopped at the first accepting state would disagree on 6 of the 13 corpus strings.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
Keywords are not decided by the automaton here: five of them spelled out as paths would add 21 states.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
There are exactly two ways to fail. No move at all from the start state: in x = 3 $ y; that is character 7.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
The second failure is a move that can never finish: the run leaves the start state and reaches no accepting state.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
In x = "abc and a newline, the quote opens at character 5 and the error is reported at character 9.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
The position in the message is where the run gave up, not where the cause is. Keep both numbers.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
A string literal has no fallback: a lone quote is not a token, so the failure is reported.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
A comment does have one. The slash is a token by itself, so /* unterminated yields three tokens and no error.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
So a real lexer commits: on seeing /* it enters the comment and reports the missing */ at the end of the input.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
That is also why the same two characters mean a comment in one place and a division in another.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
Three phases, three tests. Lexical: can this character be part of any token?

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
Syntactic: may these well-formed tokens appear in this order? Semantic: do the types and names obey the rules?

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
GATE CSE 2026 Set 1 asked exactly this of three statements: S1 is the lexical error, S3 the semantic one, answer C.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
The parser sees only tokens. On 3 + 4 ; every token is fine and the statement cannot start with a number.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
Panic mode in a lexer: delete characters until one appears that can begin a token, then resume.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
On x = 3 $ y; that is one deletion, and the run continues with x = 3 y ;.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
On x = @@@ 3; it is three deletions in one pass, and the run continues with x = 3 ;.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
Recovery counts characters; the report counts positions. One error can cost three characters.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
The rule needs a guard. At an unterminated literal the offending character is a newline, and whitespace can begin a token.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
So nothing is deleted, the same error returns, and the loop never ends. The guard: if a pass deletes nothing, delete the offending character.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
Now one deletion, one report, and the tokens are x = y = 2 ; — the literal has silently disappeared.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
Skipping to a delimiter instead deletes four characters on x = @ y ; z = 1; and resumes at the semicolon.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
The counting question: GATE CSE 2026 Set 2 gives the token rules and asks how many tokens a string makes.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
Nine blank-separated words, thirteen tokens. 23mm is number 23 and identifier mm.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
The rule that decides it: an identifier begins with a letter, so after digits a letter starts a new token.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
Count per word and add: one, two, one, one, two, one, two, two, one is thirteen.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
Five steps: scan with longest match, name the failing character and position, decide which failure it is, recover with a guard, report.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Four traps: counting words, calling a misplaced token lexical, reading the reported position as the cause, forgetting the comment's fallback.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Every recovery here changes characters, not tokens. Carry two counts: characters deleted and errors reported.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
And a repaired program is not a correct program: x = y = 2 ; is what the guard left, and the parser rejects it.

